WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.910
The university canteen must feed

2
00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:05.250
students a meal that meets nutrition

3
00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.910
targets at the lowest cost.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.510
Staff try menus by hand every week.

5
00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.390
A three-line model finds the

6
00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.030
best menu in milliseconds.

7
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:16.480
How?

8
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:20.350
Today: how linear programs model

9
00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.390
allocation problems, why the optimum sits

10
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.990
at a corner, and how duality turns

11
00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.330
constraints into prices students can

12
00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:28.650
interpret.

13
00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:32.350
A linear program maximises or

14
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:34.450
minimises a linear objective

15
00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.530
subject to linear constraints.

16
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.450
The decision variables are

17
00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.330
continuous and usually non-negative.

18
00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.290
Diet, production and transport

19
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.070
problems all fit this form.

20
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.980
The constraints carve out a

21
00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.960
convex feasible region.

22
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.990
Because the objective is linear, an

23
00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:58.210
optimum, if one exists, occurs at a

24
00:00:58.210 --> 00:00:58.530
vertex.

25
00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.590
The simplex method walks from vertex to

26
00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.410
better vertex until none is better.

27
00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:08.770
Evaluate three x plus two

28
00:01:08.770 --> 00:01:10.390
y at each vertex.

29
00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.530
The origin gives zero, the point four

30
00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:17.910
comma zero gives twelve, three comma one

31
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.150
gives eleven, and zero comma two gives

32
00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:21.470
four.

33
00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.810
The optimum is twelve at four comma zero.

34
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.880
Every linear program has a dual, with

35
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.520
one variable per constraint.

36
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:34.280
At the optimum, both have the same value.

37
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.200
Each dual variable is a shadow price: how

38
00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.340
much the objective improves if that

39
00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.400
constraint is relaxed by one unit.

40
00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:47.060
Students often learn simplex tableaux as a

41
00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.420
ritual without seeing the geometry.

42
00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.830
Start with two-variable

43
00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:55.190
problems drawn on graph paper.

44
00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.510
Then let a solver handle larger

45
00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.550
ones, and spend the time

46
00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.670
interpreting shadow prices in context.

47
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:05.500
Quick check!

48
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.380
A linear program has a bounded,

49
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.460
non-empty feasible region.

50
00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.980
Where must an optimal solution occur?

51
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.770
At a vertex, or along an edge

52
00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.910
between two equally good vertices.

53
00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.610
That is why simplex only

54
00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:23.770
needs to visit corners.

55
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.740
In the app, open the

56
00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:27.960
linear programming lab.

57
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:30.960
Drag the objective line across the

58
00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.720
feasible region and watch where it last

59
00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:34.420
touches.

60
00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:36.350
Your turn.

61
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.950
The oven-hours constraint has

62
00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:42.270
a shadow price of five.

63
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.440
One more oven hour costs three.

64
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:49.380
Should the canteen buy it?

65
00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.490
Yes: each extra hour improves the

66
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:57.110
objective by five and costs three, a net

67
00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.490
gain of two, while the price stays valid.

68
00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.600
Remember: optima sit at vertices,

69
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:06.280
every LP has a dual, and shadow

70
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.880
prices value constraints.

71
00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.920
Try it in your next lecture:

72
00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.400
give pairs a two-variable

73
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.400
canteen problem on graph paper.

74
00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:18.380
They shade the feasible region, test

75
00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.880
every corner, and find the optimum.

76
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.860
Then reveal the solver's shadow prices and

77
00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.320
ask what they mean for the manager.
